      <section aria-labelledby="what-we-provide-heading">
        <h2 id="what-we-provide-heading">What WaterLevels.org provides</h2>
        <p>WaterLevels.org provides maps and information about water monitoring stations in the United States.</p>
        <p>The site may display information such as:</p>
        <ul role="list">
          <li>Water levels</li>
          <li>Streamflow or discharge</li>
          <li>Monitoring-station locations</li>
          <li>Current and historical readings</li>
          <li>Information about the type of measurement being reported</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          WaterLevels.org is provided free of charge. User accounts are not available, and no purchase is required to use the site.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="data-sources-heading">
        <h2 id="data-sources-heading">Data sources</h2>
        <p>
          Much of the water data displayed on WaterLevels.org comes from the United States Geological Survey, or USGS.
        </p>
        <p>
          WaterLevels.org is independently operated. It is not operated by, affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by the USGS or the United States government.
        </p>
        <p>
          We may also use maps, geographic information, or other data provided by outside services.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="data-accuracy-heading">
        <h2 id="data-accuracy-heading">Data may be incomplete or inaccurate</h2>
        <p>
          We try to display water information accurately, but we cannot guarantee that any information on WaterLevels.org is accurate, complete, current, or available at all times.
        </p>
        <p>Water-monitoring data can be affected by:</p>
        <ul role="list">
          <li>Delayed transmissions</li>
          <li>Equipment failures</li>
          <li>Incorrect sensor readings</li>
          <li>Network or software problems</li>
          <li>Changes at a monitoring location</li>
          <li>Later review or correction by the data provider</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Some data may be preliminary or provisional. A data provider may revise a reading after it has already appeared on WaterLevels.org.
        </p>
        <p>
          Information shown on WaterLevels.org may also differ from information shown directly by the original data provider.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="not-for-emergencies-heading">
        <h2 id="not-for-emergencies-heading">Not for emergencies or safety decisions</h2>
        <p>WaterLevels.org is an informational tool. It is not an emergency-warning service.</p>
        <p>Do not rely only on WaterLevels.org when making decisions involving:</p>
        <ul role="list">
          <li>Flooding</li>
          <li>Evacuation</li>
          <li>Personal or public safety</li>
          <li>Boating or navigation</li>
          <li>Property protection</li>
          <li>Emergency response</li>
          <li>Business or financial risk</li>
          <li>Any other decision that could result in injury, death, property damage, or significant loss</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For emergencies, follow instructions from local emergency-management agencies, the National Weather Service, law enforcement, fire departments, and other appropriate public authorities.
        </p>
        <p>
          When accuracy is important, confirm the information with the USGS or another authoritative source.
        </p>
      </section>

